  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMore valley and marine fog will be possible early this morning.\nAreas outside of the mountains should clear up fairly quickly \nthis morning with fog lifting around 13z or so.\n\nPersistent low amplitude troughing will remain in place over the\nregion which will help keep the pattern a bit unsettled. As a \ncold front approaches today, a weak short wave will move across \nthe area, leading to some low chances of showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly over the higher terrain.\n\nTemperatures will once again be very warm ahead of the cold\nfront with highs ranging from the upper 70s and lower 80s north,\nto the upper 80s and a few lower 90s south/along the coast.\nDewpoints will start to decrease as the front moves across the \narea but we will still see values in the mid 60s to lower 70s\nahead of the boundary. Thus, we could still some heat index\nvalues in the lower 90s across southeast New Hampshire and\ncoastal Maine. A reading or two in the mid 90s will be possible\nin the vicinity of the Midcoast. Lows will then be a little bit\ncooler than in previous nights behind the front, ranging from\nupper 50s north to the low/mid 60s south.  \n\nMonday remains warm to hot, but dewpoints will be lower behind\nthe front which will keep the heat index values subdued. Still,\nhighs will range from the mid/upper 70s north, to the mid to\nupper 80s south. By Monday afternoon a weak surface low will\ndeepen near the New Hampshire/Maine border as next shortwave\nmoves into the region. This will lead to better chances of\nscattered showers and storms late Monday into Tuesday morning.\nThe best chances (40 to 70 percent) will once again be across \nnorthern New Hampshire and the western Maine mountains.\nInstability and shear will be marginal, but some models do \nsuggest the potential for some enhanced DCAPE so some gusty \nwinds are not out of the question with this activity.\n\nChances for showers and storms may linger into Tuesday morning\nbut should be on their way out through the day as high pressure\nmoves in late. High temperatures will be a touch cooler on \nTuesday, ranging from the low/mid 70s north to the mid 80s south\n(a few upper 80s will be possible over southeast New Hampshire \nand coastal Maine). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad troughing dominates the 500 mb pattern into the first \npart of the weekend. Wednesday looks to feature more zonal flow \nmaking for a drier day and starting a cooling trend. The trough \naxis moves eastward on Thursday along with forcing from a front.\nThe brief period of southwesterly flow advects some moisture \ninto the region making this the next best chance for widespread \nshowers and storms.\n\nThe trough axis moves to the east of the area late week with \nsome ensembles bringing the upper low into the region as well. \nThe resulting northwesterly flow should continue to dry out our \nairmass and bring cooler temperatures, but if an upper low is \nnearby upslope clouds and showers would be possible. Most \nensemble members begin to build high pressure into the region by\nSaturday, but depending on the track and progressiveness of the\nupper low the mountains may still see some clouds and showers.",
